    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d460">

                <pb facs="0539" n="527" sameAs="frus:frus1941v02/pg527"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">851.85/212: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Leahy</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Vichy</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-04-03T12:00:00+02:00">April 3,
                            1941—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received 2:28 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>383. Department’s telegram 279, March 3 [<hi rendition="#italic">31</hi>], 6 p.m.
                    I saw Marshal Pétain in the presence of Admiral Darlan<anchor n="80"
                        xml:id="d460-f1"/> this morning and in as much as he always prefers to have
                    something in writing I gave him a French paraphrase of the pertinent portions of
                    the Department’s 279. He said that he would prefer to reply in writing and that
                    he would send me a memorandum. Admiral Darlan said that no instructions had been
                    sent from any French authority to damage or sabotage the French vessels now in
                    American ports. Both the Marshal and Admiral Darlan said that they will be
                    prepared to send specific instructions prohibiting such sabotage if we will give
                    assurances that the vessels in question will not be handed over to the British
                    or utilized to aid the British. Presumably the memorandum will embody this
                    suggestion.</p>
                <p>Admiral Darlan stated that he had no objection to our inspection or surveillance
                    of the ships but that he hoped we would not place guards on board.</p>
                <p>I shall telegraph immediately upon receipt of the promised memorandum.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Leahy</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d460-f1" n="80" xml:id="d460-f1-content">
                <p>Adm. Jean François Darlan, Vice President of the French Council of Ministers,
                    Minister for Foreign Affairs, Navy, and Interior.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
